The table shows the estimated offloading overhead for 14 different OpenMP methods, 
    measured in microseconds (μs) with their corresponding error margins 
        (which represent the statistical uncertainty of the measurement).

1. Simple Offload Constructs:
    For methods that perform only data mapping (e.g., target map(tofrom: a), target map(to: a), etc.), 
        both CPU and GPU show extremely low overhead (around 0.04–0.05 μs). 
    This indicates that when no substantial computation is launched, the setup cost is minimal on either platform.

2. Parallel For Construct:

    CPU: The omp parallel for construct shows an overhead of approximately 4755 μs. This high cost is likely due to 
        the overhead of thread creation and synchronization on the CPU for this microbenchmark.
    GPU: A similarly purposed construct on the GPU, however, shows a much lower overhead of around 20 μs. 
        This demonstrates that GPUs can handle massive thread parallelism more efficiently 
            when the workload is appropriately offloaded.

3. Teams-Based Constructs:

    Target Teams Parallel: On the CPU, this construct incurs an overhead of about 4253 μs, 
        while on the GPU it is lower at roughly 3122 μs.
    Target Teams Parallel (Repetitions): Similarly, the repeated teams parallel construct takes around 8609 μs on the CPU 
        compared to approximately 6383 μs on the GPU.
    Both "target teams parallel" and "target teams parallel (repetitions)" incur high overhead on both platforms 
        because they involve setting up multiple teams, launching full kernels, and synchronizing threads. 
    However, GPUs generally have lower overhead for these constructs due to their design for massive parallelism 
                and efficient thread scheduling. 
    On the CPU, the cost of creating and managing many threads can be significantly higher, especially when the workload per thread is very light.


• High Overhead Methods (Target Teams Parallel):
Two methods stand out as having a much higher overhead:
These indicate that launching a target teams parallel region incurs significant overhead. 
The complexity of initializing multiple teams and threads on a device (e.g., a GPU) can lead to these higher latencies. 
The fact that repeating the parallel region further increases the cost (as seen in method 10) 
    reinforces the idea that these constructs are more expensive to set up.

• Notable Observation – Array Section Mapping:
Comparing methods that use the “target map(to: a[0:N])” with “target map(tofrom: a[0:N])”
It is because that the data transfer paths were being optimised:
    The runtime might be detecting that the device code does not modify the data, 
    so for a "tofrom" mapping it optimised away the copy-back step. 
    In contrast, the "to" mapping followed a less optimized code path that always performs certain initialization checks.
    See MBresult/microbenchmark_7206184.out